  "response": "I’m using the repository’s crimes risk workflow to validate the hot-path finding and keep the recommendation scoped to the actual blocking calls.\n\nThe event-loop stalls come from three synchronous filesystem calls in `loadPlanSchema`: `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`.\n\nFinding evidence, verbatim:\n\n> “`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  \n> “`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  \n> “`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  \n> “lines: 12, 13, 15”  \n> “swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it”\n\nThe smallest migration is one async `readFile`, which also removes the redundant existence and stat calls:\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  try {\n    const contents = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\");\n    if (contents.length === 0) return null;\n    return JSON.parse(contents);\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (\n      error instanceof Error &&\n      \"code\" in error &&\n      error.code === \"ENOENT\"\n    ) {\n      return null;\n    }\n\n    throw error;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCallers must then use:\n\n```ts\nconst schema = await loadPlanSchema();\n```\n\nThis preserves the exported name, parameters, and resolved values. It cannot preserve the exact synchronous return type `(): unknown`: genuine asynchronous filesystem access necessarily returns a `Promise`. Keeping that exact contract would require blocking, caching/preloading, or similar symptom-hiding behavior.",
